          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework fixes the following SI coordinate and unit conventions for the\noriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space $(A,V,\\eta,C^+)$ and its inertial\ncharts ([[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]],\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]).\n\n1. **Adopted conversion constant.** The framework uses a positive real constant\n   $c$, the **speed of light in vacuum**, with the SI unit metre per second,\n   $\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s$. Its numerical value is an empirical parameter of the\n   model, not a mathematical constant, and no measurement of it is made or\n   quoted in this definition.\n2. **Coordinates.** In every inertial chart $x=(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ the temporal\n   coordinate is $x^0=ct$, where $t$ is the reading of an ideal clock at rest in\n   the frame with the SI unit second $\\mathrm s$, and $x^1,x^2,x^3$ are measured\n   in metres $\\mathrm m$ along the frame's orthonormal spatial directions\n   $e_1,e_2,e_3$. Thus all four coordinates $x^a$ carry the unit $\\mathrm m$.\n3. **Metric components.** With these coordinates the matrix of $\\eta$ in an\n   inertial chart is $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ with **dimensionless**\n   entries, and the interval $I(p,q)=\\eta(q-p,q-p)$ carries the unit\n   $\\mathrm m^2$.\n4. **Proper time of a straight segment.** For a timelike displacement $q-p$ the\n   number\n\n   $$\\tau:=c^{-1}\\sqrt{-\\eta(q-p,q-p)}$$\n\n   has the unit second $\\mathrm s$ and is the **proper time of the straight\n   timelike segment from $p$ to $q$**. The factor $c^{-1}$ converts the length\n   $\\sqrt{-\\eta(q-p,q-p)}$ in metres into a time in seconds.\n5. **Derived kinematic quantities.** For a timelike worldline parametrized by\n   its proper time $\\tau$, the four-velocity $U=\\mathrm dx/\\mathrm d\\tau$ has\n   unit $\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s$, the four-acceleration $\\mathrm dU/\\mathrm d\\tau$\n   has unit $\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s^2$, the four-momentum $mU$ of a particle of\n   mass $m$ has unit $\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s$, and the observer energy\n   has unit joule. These units are recorded here as bookkeeping; the definitions\n   of the quantities themselves belong to the later worldlines, mechanics and\n   energy-momentum pages.\n6. **Temporal-coordinate convention.** If instead a chart uses a temporal\n   coordinate $t$ in seconds as the first coordinate, then the corresponding\n   metric component is $g_{tt}=-c^2$ with unit\n   $\\mathrm m^2/\\mathrm s^2$, the mixed time-space components acquire the unit\n   $\\mathrm m/\\mathrm s$, and formulas derived in one convention are not reused\n   unchanged in the other.\n7. **Natural units.** Natural units with $c=1$ may be used only together with an\n   explicit restoration table for $c$; a value $c=1$ is then a convention of\n   presentation, not a claim that the empirical constant equals the number $1$.\n\n**Status.** These are conventions and dimensional bookkeeping for the model.\nThe numerical value of $c$ is empirical; SI unit status does not make any\nquantity primitive in the theory, and the choice of unit system does not add\nphysical content to the affine Minkowski model.",
