          "source": "def-sr-operational-radar-protocol",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Adopt the ideal-clock and ideal-light assumptions of [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Let $x:I\\to A$ be a future timelike worldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]) with proper-time function $\\tau$ ([[def-sr-proper-time]]), and let $s_-<s_+$ be parameter values in $I$. Suppose an event $q$ and the events $e_-:=x(s_-)$, $e_+:=x(s_+)$ satisfy: $q-e_-$ is future null and $e_+-q$ is future null ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]], [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]]). Under the adopted ideal-light assumption an ideal ray is emitted at $e_-$, reaches $q$, and an ideal ray emitted at $q$ reaches $e_+$; the event $q$ is idealized as an instantaneous reflection that changes nothing else. The **radar protocol** of the observer assigns to $q$\n\n(i) the **radar time** $\\tau_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q):=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\tau(s_-)+\\tau(s_+)\\bigr)$, the ideal clock's reading midway between emission and reception, and\n\n(ii) the **radar distance** $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q):=\\tfrac{c}{2}\\bigl(\\tau(s_+)-\\tau(s_-)\\bigr)$, half the round-trip light time multiplied by $c$.\n\nTwo events measured by the same observer are **radar-simultaneous** when their radar times agree. The assignment does not change under increasing reparametrizations of the observer's worldline, because the proper-time function does not ([[lem-sr-proper-time-reparametrization-invariance]]).\n\n**Conventions and scope.** (a) The protocol is a definition of an operational assignment conditional on the existence of the emission, reflection and reception events, and it assumes an available ideal reflector and ideal apparatus. (b) The split of the round trip into a \"time\" and a \"distance\" uses the symmetric convention in which half of the round-trip time is assigned to the outgoing and half to the returning segment; the protocol determines the round-trip quantity $\\tau(s_+)-\\tau(s_-)$ operationally, while the derived one-way assignment is a convention and does not measure a one-way light speed independently of a synchronization convention. (c) Nothing is claimed about accelerated observers, dispersive or absorbing media, finite-resolution apparatus, global single-valuedness or detector models, and no measurement outcome, statistical inference or empirical comparison is asserted.",
