          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the special-relativity framework the following\ntwo statements, with the SI coordinate and unit conventions of\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]].\n\n**(i) Ideal clocks.** Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval and let\n$x:I\\to A$ be a curve whose coordinate representation in an inertial chart is\nof class $C^1$ ([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]) with\neverywhere future timelike coordinate derivative. An **ideal clock** carried\nalong $x$ reads, between parameters $a<b$ in $I$,\n\n$$\\sigma(b)-\\sigma(a)=c^{-1}\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-\\eta\\bigl(x'(s),x'(s)\\bigr)}\\,\\mathrm ds .$$\n\nThere is no additional acceleration-dependent term in the reading: the reading\ndepends only on the curve and the interval, not on the acceleration\n(**clock hypothesis**). The integrand is a positive continuous function of $s$\non the compact interval $[a,b]$, so the integral is a well-defined Riemann\nintegral.\n\n**(ii) Ideal light.** In vacuum, the **ideal light rays** are exactly the future\nnull straight lines\n\n$$\\{\\,p+\\lambda v:\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\},\\qquad p\\in A,\\quad v\\in V\\ \\text{future null},$$\n\nparametrized at $p$ by $\\lambda=0$. In every inertial chart $x$, such a ray has\n\n$$x(\\lambda)=x(p)+\\lambda\\,(v^0,v^1,v^2,v^3),$$\n\nand because $v$ is future null the components satisfy $v^0=\\lvert v_{\\text{vec}}\\rvert>0$\nby the orthonormal-coordinate description of the closed cones\n([[lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components]]): hence the spatial coordinate\nvelocity of the ray has magnitude $\\lvert v_{\\text{vec}}\\rvert/v^0\\cdot c=c$ in\n**every** inertial chart, with $x^0=ct$. The adopted conversion constant $c$ is\ntherefore the same in all inertial charts; its numerical value is an empirical\nparameter of the model.",
