          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt [[post-sr-inertial-covariance]], [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]] and [[post-sr-causal-signal-propagation]], and the SI conventions of [[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]] ($x^0 = c t$). Let $x = (x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ be the chart of an inertial frame ([[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]) and let $W := \\{x^1 > |x^0|\\}$ be the right wedge. Let $\\Phi$ and $n$ be the Rindler chart and congruence of [[thm-sr-fermi-walker-and-rigid-observer-examples]] (b): $\\Phi(\\theta,\\rho,y,z) = (\\rho \\sinh \\theta, \\rho \\cosh \\theta, y, z)$ on $\\mathbb R \\times (0,\\infty) \\times \\mathbb R^2$ with inverse $\\rho = \\sqrt{(x^1)^2 - (x^0)^2}$, $\\theta = \\frac12 \\log((x^1+x^0)/(x^1-x^0))$, pullback metric $-\\rho^2 d\\theta^2 + d\\rho^2 + dy^2 + dz^2$, and acceleration field $a_{\\mathrm{cg}} = \\nabla_n n = \\rho^{-1} \\partial_\\rho$. Fix $\\rho_0 > 0$ and real $y_0,z_0$, and let $O$ be the congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_0$, $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$, parametrized by its proper time $\\tau$ with $\\theta = c \\tau/\\rho_0$; by [[def-sr-proper-acceleration]] its proper acceleration is $c^2/\\rho_0$. Write $U(q) := q^0 + q^1$ and $V(q) := q^0 - q^1$ for events $q$, so that $U = \\rho e^{\\theta}$ and $V = -\\rho e^{-\\theta}$ on $W$, and write $\\le$ and $J^+$, $J^-$ for the causal reachability relations of [[def-sr-causal-reachability-in-affine-minkowski-space]]. Then, conditionally on the adopted model:\n\n(i) **Chart domain and light rays.** $\\Phi$ is a smooth bijection of $\\mathbb R \\times (0,\\infty) \\times \\mathbb R^2$ onto $W$ with the stated smooth inverse, so the chart domain is exactly $W$ and $\\rho = 0$ is not part of its coordinate domain. The boundary of $W$ in the affine model is $\\{x^1 = |x^0|\\}$, with arbitrary $x^2,x^3$: it is the union of the two null half-planes $\\{x^1 = x^0 \\ge 0\\}$ and $\\{x^1 = -x^0 \\ge 0\\}$. These half-planes are portions of the full null planes appearing as causal horizons in (ii); the full planes are not the wedge boundary. Along the worldline $\\rho = \\rho_0$ one has $V = -\\rho_0 e^{-\\theta} \\to 0^-$ and $U \\to +\\infty$ as $\\theta \\to +\\infty$, while $U = \\rho_0 e^{\\theta} \\to 0^+$ and $V \\to -\\infty$ as $\\theta \\to -\\infty$. The future null straight lines of the model that lie in the plane $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$ and meet $O$ are exactly the lines of constant $V$ (on which $d\\theta = +d\\rho/\\rho$) and the lines of constant $U$ (on which $d\\theta = -d\\rho/\\rho$).\n\n(ii) **Causal horizons.** An event $q$ satisfies $q \\le p$ for some event $p$ of $O$ if and only if $V(q) < 0$; an event $q$ satisfies $p \\le q$ for some event $p$ of $O$ if and only if $U(q) > 0$. Consequently no event with $V(q) \\ge 0$ can ever influence $O$; this set contains the null plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$, the left wedge $\\{x^1 < -|x^0|\\}$ and the future wedge $\\{x^0 > |x^1|\\}$; and no event with $U(q) \\le 0$ can ever be influenced by $O$, a set containing the null plane $\\{x^1 = -x^0\\}$, the left wedge and the past wedge $\\{x^0 < -|x^1|\\}$. The null plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$ is the boundary of the causal past of $O$ (the future horizon: the surface its worldline approaches as $\\theta \\to +\\infty$), and the null plane $\\{x^1 = -x^0\\}$ is the boundary of the causal future of $O$ (the past horizon); every event of the left wedge is causally unrelated to $O$ in both directions. The same criteria with $O$ any other congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_1$, $y = y_1$, $z = z_1$ hold verbatim: the constants $\\rho_0$, $y_0$, $z_0$ do not enter.\n\n(iii) **Radar time and distance to a coaxial congruence reflector.** Fix $\\rho_m > 0$ with $\\rho_m \\ne \\rho_0$ and let $R_m$ be the congruence worldline $\\rho = \\rho_m$, $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$ (a second uniformly accelerated observer, coaxial with $O$, with proper acceleration $c^2/\\rho_m$). Let $e_-$ be the event of $O$ at parameter $\\theta_e$ (so $\\tau(e_-) = \\rho_0 \\theta_e/c$ up to the base point of $\\tau$), let $q_m$ be the event of $R_m$ lying on the future null straight line from $e_-$ in the plane $y = y_0$, $z = z_0$, and let $e_+$ be the event of $O$ lying on the future null straight line from $q_m$; the reflection at $q_m$ is idealized as instantaneous and as leaving $R_m$'s motion unchanged, as in [[def-sr-operational-radar-protocol]]. Then $e_-, q_m, e_+$ exist uniquely, the Rindler time of $q_m$ is $\\theta_m = \\theta_e + |\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$, the return event satisfies $\\theta(e_+) = \\theta_e + 2|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ and $(\\theta_e + \\theta(e_+))/2 = \\theta_m$, so the round trip lasts $2|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ in Rindler time and $(2\\rho_0/c)|\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$ in $O$'s proper time. The radar protocol therefore assigns to $q_m$ the radar time $\\tau_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q_m) = (\\rho_0/c) \\theta_m$ plus the base constant of $\\tau$ and the radar distance $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rad}}(q_m) = \\rho_0 |\\log(\\rho_m/\\rho_0)|$; within this coaxial family the radar-time level sets are exactly the Rindler simultaneity slices $\\theta = \\mathrm{const}$, i.e. the rest slices of the congruence ([[def-sr-simultaneity-slice-and-rest-distance]]). The radar distance is not the rest distance: in the slice $\\theta = \\theta_m$ the two worldlines are at rest distance $|\\rho_m - \\rho_0|$, whereas for $\\rho_m = 2\\rho_0$ the radar distance is $\\rho_0 \\log 2 < \\rho_0$, because $\\log$ is strictly increasing and $e > 2$ implies $\\log 2 < \\log e = 1$ ([[cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three]], [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]). For every distinct positive reflector radius the notions differ: with $r = \\rho_m/\\rho_0$, one has $\\log r < r - 1$ when $r > 1$ and $-\\log r > 1 - r$ when $0 < r < 1$. The two distance formulas agree only at $r = 1$, where both have the continuous degenerate value zero. The coincident case is not a nonzero emission-reflection-reception triple of the protocol, whose distinct-reflector hypothesis remains in force. As $\\rho_m \\to 0^+$ the round-trip proper time and the radar distance grow without bound while the reflector's proper acceleration $c^2/\\rho_m$ diverges; as $\\rho_m \\to +\\infty$ the round-trip proper time and the radar distance also grow without bound while the reflector's proper acceleration tends to $0$.\n\n(iv) **Restrictions of the protocol.** No event with $V \\ge 0$ --- in particular no event of the horizon plane $\\{x^1 = x^0\\}$, of the left wedge or of the future wedge --- can be assigned a radar time or radar distance by this protocol from $O$, because by (ii) it cannot causally precede any reception event on $O$: the required reflection-to-reception leg is impossible. Separately, every event with $U \\le 0$ lacks an emission-to-reflection leg from $O$. Thus any event admitting this radar triple must satisfy $U > 0$ and $V < 0$, equivalently it must belong to $W$; the horizon is at infinite radar distance in the limit $\\rho_m \\to 0^+$. For example $q = x^{-1}(2\\rho_0,0,y_0,z_0)$ has $U(q) = V(q) = 2\\rho_0 > 0$. It is causally after $O$ at $\\theta = 0$, since the displacement is $(2\\rho_0,-\\rho_0,0,0)$, future timelike, and it even lies on a future null ray from $O$ at $\\theta = \\log 2$, with displacement $(5\\rho_0/4,-5\\rho_0/4,0,0)$. Nevertheless it cannot send a causal return to $O$, so it receives no radar assignment from $O$. The protocol therefore does not extend to a global coordinate system of $A$.\n\n(v) **Scope.** All clauses are conditional computations and readings in flat classical spacetime with ideal clocks, ideal light rays and ideal reflectors, for the specified congruence worldlines. They are not empirical claims and contain no measurement, apparatus, source, detector or statistical content; gravitation, media, dispersion, finite resolution, quantum effects and material models of the reflector are excluded. No choice principle is used.",
